<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="133" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>C11 — secure provisioning can no longer lock the creator out: share before the owner move, verify the share survived, compensate on failure, and make a partially-provisioned record resumable instead of 409 — while a genuinely provisioned record still gets 409 already_provisioned</title>
    <phase>Register-driven (authored 2026-09-30, session 27; defect C11)</phase>
    <gate>after-144 (task 144 rewrites Step 3 of the same endpoint to a named non-default owner team and generalizes provisioning to sprk_matter and sprk_workassignment; this task's fix must cover every root type provisioning handles once 144 lands)</gate>
    <status>completed-with-escalation</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Authorization-boundary .cs change (tags bff-api, auth, security) that reorders mutations on the secure-record isolation path and changes the idempotency contract of a live endpoint.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Security isolation boundary: the fix trades between "locked box" (fail closed, unusable) and "back in the creator's BU" (usable, not isolated) on every failure branch, and must keep the 409 idempotency guarantee intact for completed provisions. Requires judgment on Dataverse share/assign semantics that must be proven live.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Modifies ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs, which six other register tasks also edit: 144 (C10 part 1 — rewrites Step 3, generalizes roots; hard dep), 139 (C4 — CreatorAccessRights / CollaboratorAccessRights values and the :158-181 rationale), 143 (No Access guard on the creator/colleague share sites :726/:754), 148 (backfill at provision), 149 (sharee sync from the provisioning shares) and 150 (sprk_issecure set as provisioning's FIRST mutation). Also edits the shared provisioning test fixture, provisioningService.ts (also edited by 150) and CreateProjectWizard.tsx (also edited by 150). Sequencing: 144 → 133, then 148/149 (which already dep on 146) build on 133's resume/ensure structure; 139, 143 and 150 may land on either side of 133 — whichever lands second rebases and re-runs ProvisionProjectIdempotencyTests + SecureProjectShareTests + provisioningService.test.ts.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>144</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, auth, security, dataverse, testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>6-10 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Session 26 defect C11 (MEDIUM, availability/integrity, no disclosure; 3/3 skeptics confirmed). Task 068 found it and mitigated with client copy only; task 061 closed while claiming a share failure "orphans nothing". No issue existed at authoring.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Provisioning a secure record moves ownership to a memberless team and only THEN shares the record back to the person who created it. If that share fails, the creator is locked out, the endpoint says "retry", and a retry is impossible: the creator now fails the Write gate (403) and anyone else hits 409 already_provisioned. Re-order and harden the flow so no single failure strands the creator, make every partial state honestly resumable, correct every false comment and message, and keep the 409 for records that really are fully provisioned.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer on the Dataverse row-security isolation boundary — Dataverse ownership (AssignRequest via ownerid bind), principalobjectaccess sharing (GrantAccess / RetrieveSharedPrincipalsAndAccess), and the BFF ExternalAccess endpoints. Fail closed (ADR-003); verify every write by read-back.</role>

  <goal>After this task, for every root type `/provision-project` handles after task 144: (1) the creator's identity is resolved before any mutation; (2) the creator's POA share is established before or verified immediately after the owner move, so the owner move never completes while the creator has no share; (3) a creator-share failure leaves the record in its exact pre-call ownership, and the response says so truthfully, so the same caller can retry and succeed; (4) a record owned by the secure owner team but with no container recorded is RESUMED (creator share ensured, container created and recorded), not refused; (5) a record owned by the secure owner team WITH a container recorded still returns 409 already_provisioned and writes nothing; (6) every comment, ProblemDetails detail and client message on this path describes what actually happened; (7) every state the server calls retryable can actually be retried from the wizard; (8) the behaviour is proven live in dev.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>Verified against the working tree 2026-09-30 (anchors are pre-task-144; re-derive them after 144 merges).

THE DEFECT. `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ExternalAccess/ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs` runs, in order: Step 4 idempotency marker `IsOwnedBy(ownerTeamId)` → 409 (`:445-472`, marker at `:1017-1018`); Step 5 owner move to the Secure Record owner team, verified by read-back (`:485-508`, `AssignOwnerToSecureTeamAsync` `:597-663`); Step 5.5 WhoAmI + app-only `GrantAccessAsync` of `CreatorAccessRights` to the CALLER (`:517-521`, `ShareToCreatorAndPrincipalsAsync` `:696-779`); Step 6 container; Step 7 record container. If Step 5.5 fails, the handler returns 403 `creator_unresolved` (`:707-722`) or 500 `creator_share_failed` (`:724-745`) with ownership already moved and deliberately not rolled back (`:29-31`, `:785-788`). The owner team is memberless by design, so the creator can no longer open the record.

THE CLAIMED RETRY IS IMPOSSIBLE — three independent reasons.
  1. The creator's retry is refused 403 `delegation_write_required`: the whole `/api/v1/external-access` group is gated by `DelegationRuleFilter` (`ExternalAccessEndpoints.cs:123-126`, route at `:172`), which requires the CALLER's OBO Write on the project (`DelegationRuleFilter.cs:86`, `:170-179`; project target at `:239-241`). The creator no longer has Write.
  2. Any caller who does pass the filter hits Step 4's marker: 409 `already_provisioned`.
  3. Even without both, Step 5.5 shares to the CURRENT caller (WhoAmI, `:705-707`), not the original creator — an admin's retry would share the record to the admin.
Recovery today is manual only: share in Dataverse, or reassign the owner and re-run.

FALSE STATEMENTS ON THIS PATH (all must be corrected):
  • `:512-516` "a share failure leaves NOTHING orphaned … the state it fails into is 'not provisioned', which a retry resolves cleanly" — false: ownership has moved.
  • `:742-743` detail "retry once the share path is healthy" — false for the reasons above.
  • `:716-721` creator_unresolved detail "Provisioning was stopped rather than leaving a secure project that nobody can open" — false: it runs after the move, so it DOES leave one.
  • `:500-503` detail "Nothing has been provisioned" on `OwnerAssignmentOutcome.Failed` — false when the PATCH committed and only the read-back threw (`:648-656`).
  • `:463-466` 409 detail "Reassign the project's owner and retry" — true only for an administrator; the creator cannot act on it.
  • `:996-1009` `ProjectRow` remarks: the wizard "writes sprk_containerid at CREATE time" — no longer true since task 076 (`src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/services/EntityCreationService.ts:319-334`: "sprk_containerid is now written by exactly one authority: the server"). The same stale claim also sits in the class remarks at `:61-64` and in `RecordContainerOnProjectAsync`'s remarks and log line (`:860-864`, `:876-880`: "Any value already here … came from the wizard's business-unit cascade").
  • `:962-967` `sprk_containerid` "is NOT a reliable sign of provisioning … must never be used as the marker" and `:1011-1015` (residual-edge remark) — both become false the moment this task makes "team-owned AND container recorded" the 409 marker; rewrite them to state the new marker and WHY it is now sound (task 076 made Step 7 the sole writer).
  • `:29-31` "There is deliberately no rollback path" — false once compensation exists; narrow it to container failures (see adr-tensions).
  • `provisioningService.ts:161-167` says owner_assignment_failed / _not_applied fall through to copy asserting the endpoint's "Nothing has been provisioned" — must follow the corrected `:500-503` detail.
  • `CreateProjectWizard/SecureProjectSection.tsx:173-214` lists what provisioning does "in the order the server does it" and maps each item to endpoint step numbers ("Endpoint steps 2, 3 and 5", "Endpoint step 5.5", "steps 6 and 7"); it pins itself to "code wins" (`:177-178`), so it must follow the reorder (and any user-visible copy that implies an order must stay true).
  • `src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/components/CreateProjectWizard/CreateProjectWizard.tsx:708-709` "a refusal means nothing was moved" — false for the share-failure refusals.
  • `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Dataverse/RecordContainerResolver.cs:250-254` tells the operator to "Provision … (POST /api/external/projects/provision)" — that route does not exist; the endpoint is `POST /api/v1/external-access/provision-project`.
Client copy that encodes today's broken behaviour and must be re-aligned with the fixed server states: `provisioningService.ts:176-192` (the "never advise try again once ownership may have moved" rule), `:205-216` (hedged already-provisioned copy), `:234-240` (share-failed copy "you may not be able to open it yet").

WHY "RESUME" IS NOW SAFE TO KEY ON AN EMPTY CONTAINER. Step 7 is the only writer of `sprk_containerid` (task 076, above). So "owned by the secure owner team AND `sprk_containerid` non-empty" means Step 7 completed = genuinely provisioned → keep the 409. "Owned by the team AND `sprk_containerid` empty" means Steps 5.5/6/7 did not all complete → resumable. A container created by a Step 6 that was followed by a failed Step 7 is unrecorded and therefore cannot receive content: uploads to a secure record with no own container fail closed (`RecordContainerResolver.cs:244-266`), so a resumed run that creates a fresh container orphans at most an EMPTY container, which the failed Step-7 response already named (`:551-559`).

WHO "THE CREATOR" IS ON RESUME. The normal path keeps today's contract: the share target is the caller, identified from their own token via WhoAmI, never from the request body (`:676-679`). On resume the original caller's id is not stored anywhere today. The persisted identity used on resume is the record's `createdby` (`_createdby_value`) — stamped by Dataverse, unforgeable, and requiring no new column — PROVIDED every host of the Create Project wizard creates the row as the human user (the wizard creates via `IDataService.createRecord`, `CreateProjectWizard/projectService.ts:395`; verify per host that this is host-context `Xrm.WebApi`, not a BFF app-only create).

REUSABLE PIECES. `IDataverseRecordShareService.GetPrincipalAccessOrThrowAsync` (`Services/Access/IDataverseRecordShareService.cs:62-66`) is the complete-answer-or-throw POA read (task 063) — use it for the post-move share read-back, never the fail-soft `GetPrincipalAccessAsync`. `AssignOwnerToSecureTeamAsync`'s bind-then-read-back discipline (`:597-663`) is the pattern for the compensating reassignment. Test harness: `tests/integration/data-mutation/ExternalAccess/ProvisionProjectTestFixture.cs` (seeded projects, `FailShareForPrincipal`, `CallerSystemUserIdResolves`, recorded `Grants`/`Updates`/`CreatedContainerDisplayNames`); existing contract tests at `SecureProjectShareTests.cs:126-157` and `ProvisionProjectIdempotencyTests.cs:399-442`, `:508-535` (select pin).

DATAVERSE BEHAVIOUR THAT MUST BE PROVEN LIVE, NOT ASSUMED: (a) whether GrantAccess to the record's CURRENT owner succeeds; (b) whether that POA row survives the ownerid reassignment with its rights mask intact; (c) the value of `organization.sharetopreviousowneronassign` (task 144 also reads it and STOPs if true), which would add a second, platform-issued share to the previous owner on every assign; (d) the Assign cascade behaviour of each provisionable root's 1:N relationships (`CascadeConfiguration.Assign`) — if an assign cascades, the forward move AND the compensating move re-own children, and compensation would then move every child to the ROOT's pre-call owner rather than back to each child's own previous owner.

INTERACTION WITH SIBLING TASKS (reviewer-verified 2026-09-30):
  • Task 150 (`sprk_issecure` locked): provisioning will SET `sprk_issecure = true` as its first mutation, read-back verified (task 150 — its constraint that provisioning sets sprk_issecure as the first mutation; its step 3), instead of requiring it already true (`:279-284`). So "pre-call state" is NOT fully restorable after 150: the flag stays set. Leaving it set is the fail-CLOSED choice — a secure-flagged record with no own container refuses uploads (`RecordContainerResolver.cs:244-266`), whereas clearing it would let content land in a shared container that cannot be retracted. 150 also adds a record-level secure surface on project/matter/work assignment that calls the provisioning endpoint — which, once it exists, is a real self-service retry path.
  • Task 143 (No Access list for internal users on secure records) guards the POA share writers, naming this endpoint's creator/colleague sites (`:726`, `:754`). Every share this task adds or moves (pre-move, post-move, resume) is such a site.
  • Task 139 (C4) owns `CreatorAccessRights` / `CollaboratorAccessRights` and their rationale.
  • Tasks 148/149 add backfill and sharee-sync steps to provisioning; 148 already carries a trigger about "the C11 resumable-provisioning change". The resume path this task builds is where those steps must re-run.
  • Only the Create Project wizard calls this endpoint today (`CreateProjectWizard.tsx:691`, via `provisioningService.ts:293`); it calls once and has NO retry affordance (`:707-713`). The precedent for an in-dialog retry is `CreateProjectWizard/CloseProjectDialog.tsx:347`, `:394`. `SummarizeFilesWizard/SummarizeFilesDialog.tsx` also creates projects through the same ProjectService (task 150 POML, its line 36).</background>
