  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: ordering of mutations in the provisioning handler. Resolve the caller's systemuserid (WhoAmI) BEFORE the owner move; if it cannot be resolved, return 403 `creator_unresolved` with nothing written. Issue the creator's POA share BEFORE the owner move while the caller still holds the record (share-first) — only if the live check proves (a) and (b) in the background; after the move, read the shares back with `GetPrincipalAccessOrThrowAsync` and confirm the creator holds exactly `CreatorAccessRights`. If the live check disproves (a) or (b), drop the pre-move grant and grant immediately after the move instead; the post-move read-back and the compensation below apply in both designs.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: creator-share failure AFTER the owner move (post-move grant or read-back fails, including a read-back that throws). Compensate: reassign ownership back to the owner read in Step 1 (the pre-call owning user or team — add `_owninguser_value` / `_ownerid_value` to the Step-1 select and update the select-pin tests), and verify by read-back exactly as `AssignOwnerToSecureTeamAsync` does. Compensation also REMOVES any POA share this call issued that the pre-call share set did not contain (the pre-call set is read with `GetPrincipalAccessOrThrowAsync` before the first mutation; an unreadable pre-call set means share-first is not used for this call — grant post-move instead), so a share-first grant can never outlive a compensated run and widen the creator beyond what they held before (e.g. ShareAccess on a record that was owned by a team, not by them). If compensation is verified: return `creator_share_failed` with a detail stating the record's OWNERSHIP and SHARES are back to their pre-call state and the same caller may retry. If compensation fails or cannot be verified: return a DISTINCT reasonCode (e.g. `sdap.provision.creator_share_failed_resumable`) whose detail states the record is owned by the secure team without a creator share and names the resume path. Never report "nothing moved" unless a read-back proved it.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: the Step-4 idempotency marker. Owned by the resolved secure owner team AND `sprk_containerid` non-empty → 409 `already_provisioned`, zero writes, exactly as today (the genuinely-provisioned guarantee MUST NOT weaken). Owned by the team AND `sprk_containerid` empty → RESUME: ensure the creator share (re-grant only if the complete POA read shows it missing), then Steps 6-7. Every resume step is an idempotent "ensure" keyed on observed state, never on a step number, so task 144's and C10 part 2's added steps slot in without re-breaking resumability. The legacy per-project-BU refusal (`:425-443`) is unchanged.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: the share target on resume. Ensure the share for the record's `createdby` systemuser (enabled, non-application user). If the resume caller is that same user, that is the same share. Do NOT add a share for a resume caller who is not the creator (an administrator reaches the record through their role; widening the explicit access list is a C4 decision, not a recovery side effect). If `createdby` is disabled, is an application user, or cannot be read, the resume refuses with its own reasonCode and names the state — it never shares to the caller as a substitute.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: `DelegationRuleFilter`. Do NOT relax it for this route. With share-first plus compensation the creator keeps Write in every single-failure path, so self-service retry works through the existing gate; the only state needing an administrator is the double failure (share AND compensation), and an administrator holds Write.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: rights masks. `CreatorAccessRights` and `CollaboratorAccessRights` (`:169`, `:181`) and their meaning are owned by task 139 (C4 grant model). This task does not change either value; it may only move where the grant happens. Tests reference the constants, never a literal mask, so they survive 139 landing on either side.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: every POA share this task issues, moves or re-issues (pre-move, post-move, resume). Each is a share-writer site under task 143's No Access guard: if 143 has landed, route through its guard; if not, list the new sites in this task's note so 143's step-1 census picks them up (its census cites `:726`/`:754`, which this task moves). On RESUME, a `createdby` user who is on the record's No Access list is refused with its own reasonCode and no share — never shared to anyway.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: resume ordering with tasks 148/149. The resume path runs every "ensure" step the forward path runs — including any backfill (148) and sharee-sync (149) step present when this task executes — keyed on observed state. Do not build a resume path that skips a step the forward path performs.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: the "same caller may retry" promise must be ACTIONABLE. Client copy advises a retry only through an affordance that exists in the product at that moment. The wizard's provisioning-failure state offers a "Try securing again" action for the retryable outcomes (verified-compensation `creator_share_failed`, the resume-eligible states) that re-calls `provisionSecureProject` for the same project id, following the CloseProjectDialog retry precedent; if task 150's record-level secure surface has landed, the copy may also name it. Copy that says "you can retry" with nothing to click is the FR-31 defect class (a sentence that renders and is untrue) that task 068's rules exist to prevent.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-003">Scope: every new branch. Fail closed with a stable `sdap.provision.*` reasonCode in ProblemDetails (ADR-019). An unreadable POA list is a failure, not "share present". An unverifiable compensation is a failure, not "reverted".</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: named colleagues (`SharePrincipalIds`). Their shares stay best-effort (task 061 semantics); they are issued only after the creator's share is proven and must not change the outcome of any branch above.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Scope: tests. Extend `ProvisionProjectTestFixture` (substituted seams); no Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;, no DI-registration or ctor null-check tests. No live Dataverse in CI — live proof is the manual gate. Existing tests whose contract this task deliberately changes (`ProvisionProjectIdempotencyTests.ProvisionProject_WhenOwnershipWasClaimedButNoContainerRecorded_IsRefusedAndSaysSo` `:427`, `SecureProjectShareTests.Provisioning_WhenTheCallersIdentityCannotBeEstablished_FailsRatherThanLeavingALockedBox` `:127`, `SecureProjectShareTests.Provisioning_WhenTheCreatorsShareFails_FailsAndCreatesNoContainer` `:145`, and any other test the new contract touches) are REWRITTEN to the new contract with a one-line reason in the task note, never deleted to make the suite pass.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Scope: this task's PR. Placement: stays in the existing endpoint (no new service, no new column). Publish size measured against a fresh origin/master build from a short path, both sides Compress-Archive, equal file counts, both absolute sizes + delta reported, ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner 2026-09-30 (binding)">Scope: whole task. No scope cuts and nothing deferred — scope found during execution is added here. No Dataverse test in CI; the live gate is manual, run with the existing non-admin test users in their current BU (do not relocate users). `sprk_issecure` is locked and endpoint-managed (owner round 2 item 2; field lock is task 150). Compensation and resume NEVER clear `sprk_issecure`: a secure-requested record that failed provisioning stays flagged so its uploads fail closed (`RecordContainerResolver.cs:244-266`). This task adds no other write of the flag; if task 150 has landed, provisioning's first-mutation flag write is 150's and is left as 150 specifies (so "pre-call state" in this task means pre-call OWNERSHIP and SHARES, never the flag).</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <adr-tensions>
    <tension adr="ADR-003" rule="MUST fail closed on every error path">
      <conflict>Compensation moves a secure-flagged record back from the memberless secure team to its pre-call owner, i.e. back into an ordinary BU where Deep-read roles can see it. Read narrowly, "fail closed" could argue for leaving the record locked (isolated, unusable) instead.</conflict>
      <proposed-path>C (comply)</proposed-path>
      <rationale>Compensation restores the record's pre-call OWNERSHIP and SHARE SET — the same access state every earlier failure (Steps 1-4, the pre-move share, creator_unresolved) already leaves it in, and the state it has held since the wizard created it — while leaving `sprk_issecure` set, so uploads to it still fail closed until it is provisioned. No access decision fails open: no caller gains access they lacked before the request (any share the call added is removed). The locked alternative is the current defect. The file's own "no rollback" principle (`:29-31`, `:785-788`) is about CONTAINER failures after a successful share and remains true for them; this task rewrites those comments to state the narrower rule.</rationale>
      <reviewer-action>Code review confirms path C explicitly in the PR description; if the reviewer judges this a deviation, it escalates to path A per CLAUDE.md §6.5 before merge.</reviewer-action>
    </tension>
  </adr-tensions>

